  "query_preview": "..Wed Oct 21 through Sunday Oct 25...A broad closed low\ncontinues down the west coast Tuesday into Wednesday before\nstalling just northwest of Pt Conception Wednesday afternoon, then\nfinally sweeping south towards the region late Wednesday. Onshore\nwinds will pick up Wednesday afternoon for the usual mountains, \ndesert slopes/deserts, and marine waters. These winds don't look \nto be significantly strong, with gusts likely in the 25 to 35 mph \nrange for the most part (to around 20 mph over the coastal \nwaters). \n\nRain chances increase through the day Wednesday; the current \nforecast has widespread light to moderate rain moving through the \narea late Wednesday through Thursday morning. South-southwest flow\nas the rain band moves in will favor the San Bernardino \nMountains, where we're expecting the most overall rainfall. The\nfront looks likely to weaken slightly as it passes through, with\nlighter rain for south and east San Diego County compared to North\nCounty, Orange County, and southwest San Bernardino County. Rain \nrates will likely be steadily light to locally moderate through \nthis period.\n\nWidespread rain gives way to scattered showers Thursday as the low\ndigs south, finally sweeping right over the area Thursday morning\ninto the afternoon along with the associated vort max. This will\nbring 1000-700 MB instability of 350-500 J/kg to the area in turn\nleading to widespread 15 to 25% chance for thunderstorms for the\nmountains and areas westwards. Thunderstorms will bring the threat\nfor locally heavier rainfall as well as the usual dangerous\nlighting, gusty winds, etc. so this may be the most potentially \nimpactful period during this event. Snow levels will drop \nThursday-Friday, but only to about 8000 feet, so it's unlikely \nthat any area but the highest mountain peaks receive snow. \n\nShowers gradually end sometime between late Thursday and Friday\nmorning. All in all, total rainfall for the region looks to be in\nthe range of 0.7\" to about an inch for North County San Diego,\nOrange County, and the northwest Inland Empire, with 1\" to 1.3\" \nlikely for the San Bernardino Mountains. South and east county San\nDiego (including the mountains) as well as the southeast Inland \nEmpire and Riverside Mountains likely to get 0.4\" to 0.7\", with\n0.2\" to 0.5\" in the High Desert and likely under 0.1\" for the low\ndeserts. We've gotten an excellent head start on the Water Year\n(which started Oct 1st), and this event will keep the trend going. \n\nModels diverge on the location of the low going into next weekend,\nwith some showing the low stalling near AZ over the weekend and \nothers a smooth exit eastwards. In either case, temperatures will\nstart to rebound Friday through Sunday, and winds will likely turn\noffshore for Saturday-Sunday. The strength of these winds varies\nquite a bit between ensemble model members, with most of them at\nthis point looking pretty weak, but confidence will increase as\nthe week progresses. Looking out towards the end of the month,\nsome form of troughing is likely on the far horizon. The current\nClimate Predication Center's outlook for October 26 through\nNovember 1 in southern California is for near normal temperatures\nand leaning above average precipitation (50-60% chance).",
